---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
---

<Layout title="Sideboard Analysis - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Sideboard Analysis"
        description="An analytical card-by-card breakdown of the Premodern Replenish sideboard architecture, evaluating Tier 1 tournament staples, high-leverage metagame bullets, and modular utility slots."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/opalescence.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Opalescence"
        imageCaption="Opalescence by John Avon"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Tier 1: Core Tournament Staples" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            The foundational reactive and transformative pillars of competitive
            Premodern Replenish sideboards. These slots address universal format
            imbalances and belong in virtually every optimized 75.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Exalted Angel"
            image="creatures/exalted_angel.jpg"
            badge="2–3 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                The quintessential post-sideboard hammer, introduced across
                virtually every non-combo matchup. Against red aggression, even
                deploying it as a face-down morph blocker that absorbs a burn
                spell preserves critical life. Against control, it demands
                physical removal that opponents frequently trimmed after Game 1.
                Against mana-denial builds (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Terravore">Terrageddon</a
                >), establishing an early Angel clock consistently deters
                opponents from executing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Armageddon">Armageddon</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>The Transformational Advantage:</strong> Because opponents
                predictably side out spot removal in favor of enchantment sweepers
                and graveyard extraction (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >), an early morph transition frequently dominates UW Control,
                Rock, and Ponza while they clutch inert hate pieces.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Defense Grid"
            image="artifacts/defense_grid.jpg"
            badge="2–3 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The premier resource equalizer against permission-heavy
                structures. By forcing opponents to pay an additional three mana
                to play spells during your turn, it neutralizes countermagic and
                allows your four-mana combo setups to deploy uninhibited.
                Furthermore, it operates seamlessly alongside cycling <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                > on opposing end steps.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Hydroblast / Blue Elemental Blast"
            image="instants/hydroblast.jpg"
            badge="3–4 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The cleanest one-mana answer to rapid red clocks and red
                disruption suites. Whether countering an inbound <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Pyroblast">Pyroblast</a
                > targeting your combo pieces or destroying a lethal <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Piledriver">Goblin Piledriver</a
                > on combat declaration, Hydroblast represents indispensable defensive
                velocity.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Wrath of God"
            image="sorceries/wrath_of_god.webp"
            badge="1–2 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The foundational sweeping reset against swarming tribal
                formations and accelerated midrange boards. In regional
                environments saturated with Elves, Goblins, or Green White aggro
                shells, supplementing spot removal with board sweepers buys the
                necessary breathing room to assemble your enchantment lock.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Chill"
            image="enchantments/chill.jpg"
            badge="2–3 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                An absolute tournament powerhouse against aggressive red decks,
                Sligh, and Burn. By doubling the casting cost of every red
                spell, it completely paralyzes early burn velocity and renders
                high-cost threats untranslatable. Because it exists as an
                enchantment, any discarded copies naturally recur during
                late-game combo loops.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Disenchant"
            image="instants/disenchant.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>The Seal/Disenchant Split:</strong> With the dominance of
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Aura of Silence"
                    >Aura of Silence</a
                > across contemporary tournaments, relying exclusively on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > post-board presents vulnerability to two-mana static taxation. Maintaining
                1 or 2 copies of <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > provides immune, instant-speed interaction that cleanly bypasses
                Aura's cost penalty.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Tier 2: High-Leverage Metagame Bullets" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            Targeted weapons and defensive architectures calibrated to
            neutralize specific archetype saturations and aggressive post-board
            hate suites.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Anti-Red Defense: Sphere of Law vs. Warmth vs. CoP: Red"
            image="enchantments/sphere_of_law.jpg"
            badge="Theory & Comparison"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Selecting the correct red defense suite requires rigorous
                competitive analysis of your mana tolerances and anticipated
                damage profiles:
            </p>
            <ul style="margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Sphere of Law (Recommended):</strong> Simply the superior
                    four-mana solution against both Goblins and Sligh. Subtracting
                    two points of damage passively from <em>every</em> source creates
                    an unbreachable defensive wall without consuming ongoing mana.
                    The acknowledged tradeoff is that aggressive red pilots will heavily
                    side in <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a
                    > as counter-hate, requiring prompt countermagic readiness.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
                    <strong>Warmth:</strong> Exceptional against spell-slinging Sligh
                    builds where gaining two life per red spell nullifies burn curves.
                    However, it is structurally flawed against Goblins—recurring combat
                    damage from aggressive creatures rapidly overwhelms the temporary
                    lifegain buffer, rendering Warmth severely one-dimensional.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <strong>Circle of Protection: Red:</strong> While universally
                    protective, CoP is fatally <em>"mana hungry."</em> In our proactive
                    combo-control architecture, where mana utilization is extremely
                    tight for <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                    > cycling and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                    > velocity, we lack the luxury of remaining untapped like a passive
                    draw-go deck. Continually taxing your own generic mana directly
                    stifles combo assembly, rendering CoP sub-optimal here.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Annul" badge="1–2 Side · Instant Answer">
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                An exceptionally versatile one-mana permission bullet that
                solves problem permanents directly on the stack—neutralizing
                static taxers before they constrain our mana or when waiting to
                crack an on-board <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > carries lethal timing risks.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Exhaustive Premodern Target Matrix:</strong> Maintaining Annul
                in your 15 yields super-efficient 1-for-1 trades against format-defining
                gamebreakers: <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Resistance"
                    >Sphere of Resistance</a
                >, opposing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > / <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                > (crucial in mirror battles), <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Standstill">Standstill</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Aluren"
                    >Aluren</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Dream Halls"
                    >Dream Halls</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Mox Diamond"
                    >Mox Diamond</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Cursed Totem"
                    >Cursed Totem</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sterling Grove">Sterling Grove</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Zombie Infestation">Zombie Infestation</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Devourer">Phyrexian Devourer</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Altar of Dementia">Altar of Dementia</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Survival of the Fittest"
                    >Survival of the Fittest</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tormod's Crypt"
            image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg"
            badge="1–3 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The zero-mana benchmark for graveyard interaction. Critical
                against accelerated Reanimator loops, explosive Phyrexian
                Devourer combo structures, and tactical Replenish mirrors. In
                mirror matches, mastery requires clinical timing: preserve your
                Crypt untapped on the table and sacrifice it strictly in
                response to an opponent's terminal <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > resolution or an <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > activation on the stack, obliterating their setup while leaving
                your own graveyard architecture intact.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Nevinyrral's Disk"
            image="artifacts/nevinyrrals_disk.jpg"
            badge="0–2 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The premier universal reset button against intricate permanent
                locks, heavy midrange boards, and resolved anti-combo artifacts.
                In protracted attrition battles (e.g., The Rock, UW Control),
                detonating an active Disk not only wipes opposing creatures and
                hate pieces simultaneously, but milimetrically drops your own
                fielded enchantments directly into your graveyard—preparing a
                devastating follow-up <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > onto a completely clear board.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Light of Day"
            image="enchantments/light_of_day.jpg"
            badge="1–2 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                The ultimate structural lockdown against black creature-driven
                archetypes. Against Suicide Black, Psychatog, or aggressive The
                Rock formations, deploying a resolved <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Light of Day">Light of Day</a
                > establishes an absolute prohibition: black creatures are permanently
                forbidden from attacking or blocking. Furthermore, its four-mana enchantment
                card type ensures seamless recursion with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > if stripped early by opposing discard suites.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Orim's Chant & Abeyance"
            image="instants/orims_chant.webp"
            badge="1–2 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                High-leverage white instants that force open safe interaction
                windows. Casting <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Orim's Chant">Orim's Chant</a
                > (kicked or unkicked) during an opponent's upkeep halts aggressive
                deployment turns, while leading with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                > prior to casting Replenish completely suppresses opposing countermagic
                and troublesome activated abilities (such as <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
                >) while instantly replacing itself via cantrip draw.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Alter Reality"
            image="instants/alter_reality.jpg"
            badge="0–1 Side"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Highly precise regional tech deployed specifically to counter <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                > from black disruptive piles. By modulating the word "White" to "Black"
                on their resolved Gloom, the tax reverses entirely onto the opponent's
                own spell curve. Its embedded Flashback cost ensures functional utility
                even under discarded constraints.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Tier 3: Niche & Modular Technology" titleTag="h2">
        <p
            style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-20); margin-bottom: 2rem;"
        >
            Specialized tactical tools, surgical interactions, and modular
            candidate slots designed for unique meta considerations and
            individualized playstyle preferences.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Seal of Removal & Shackles (Proactive Creature Solutions)"
            badge="Modular Tech"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Low-cost enchantment-based removal options that excel against
                explosive early threats like Turn 1 <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Goblin Lackey">Goblin Lackey</a
                > or high-pressure threats possessing protection from white. Deploying
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Seal of Removal"
                    >Seal of Removal</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Shackles"
                    >Shackles</a
                > buys vital setup turns for combo assembly while contributing recurring
                permanent velocity to your graveyard pool.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                <strong>Strategic Critique & Tradeoff:</strong> Unlike terminal exile
                via spot removal (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                >), relying on permanent enchantment constraints carries an
                acknowledged vulnerability: opponents running post-board answers
                (<a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Naturalize"
                    >Naturalize</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                >) can destroy your restraining piece post-combat or during end
                steps, instantly unbinding their threat and neutralizing your
                previous removal investment.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Spiritual Focus" badge="Anti-Discard Tech">
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                A specialized defensive enchantment that harshly penalizes
                aggressive black discard architectures by drawing two cards
                whenever an opponent forces a hand drop via <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Hymn to Tourach">Hymn to Tourach</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gerrard's Verdict">Gerrard's Verdict</a
                >, or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Duress"
                    >Duress</a
                >. While select veteran pilots value its ability to out-grind
                heavy attrition loops, it remains inherently slow and
                passive—frequently underperforming in fast-paced tempo
                environments where initiative is paramount.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Stifle"
            image="stiflenought/stifle.jpg"
            badge="Surgical Tech"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                Historically a mandatory staple during competitive eras
                dominated by High Tide and Reset combo shells, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Stifle">Stifle</a
                > exists today as a foundational relic in traditional Premodern Replenish
                lists. However, it retains surgical tournament utility in specialized
                fields—instantly shutting off <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Dreadnought"
                    >Phyrexian Dreadnought</a
                > deployment triggers, halting Storm mechanics, or completely blowing
                out an opponent attempting to activate <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > against your graveyard pool.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Secondary Tactical Toolbox" badge="Flex Pool">
            <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                A curated overview of modular candidates available when
                tailoring your master 85–90 card pool for specific local
                environments:
            </p>
            <ul
                style="margin-top: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 0; padding-left: 1.25rem;"
            >
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
                    <strong>Aura of Silence:</strong> Asymmetrical taxation and recurring
                    enchantment/artifact control that cripples opposing mirror alignments
                    and Sol-land artifact ramps.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
                    <strong>Library of Leng:</strong> Preserves carefully sculpted
                    hands against targeted discard while bypassing maximum hand size
                    constraints during heavy draw sequences.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
                    <strong>Mana Short:</strong> A devastating end-step tap-down bullet
                    against heavy permission architectures, clearing all opposing
                    blue mana before initiating your main-phase combo.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
                    <strong>Voidmage Apprentice:</strong> Uncounterable morph countermagic
                    that cleanly circumvents opposing spell defenses and permission
                    locks.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0.4rem;">
                    <strong>Hibernation:</strong> An instant-speed asymmetric sweeper
                    that obliterates green-heavy table presences, resetting Rock permanents,
                    Survival engines, and Enchantress boards simultaneously.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <strong>Arcane Laboratory:</strong> Absolute structural prohibition
                    against chain-casting combo structures and velocity engines.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Navigating Opposing Sideboard Hate" titleTag="h2">
        <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
            Once you have constructed your sideboard architecture and mastered
            your strategic transformations, the ultimate test of competitive
            proficiency is anticipating and out-sequencing your opponent's
            targeted hate in Games 2 and 3. For a comprehensive, card-by-card
            masterclass on defeating graveyard extraction, taxing structures,
            and enchantment sweepers, proceed to our dedicated <a
                href="/playing-through-hate"
                style="color: var(--w-color-info); text-decoration: underline; font-weight: bold;"
                >Playing Through Hate analysis</a
            >.
        </p>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
